// The boss journal: Queen Skitterra, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).skitterra = {
  // the first time this hero meets her (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "Queen Skitterra. A great spider in a cave of pillars and silk, and the floor itself works for her. Bad footing everywhere. I kept her face on me and the line behind. Will hold.",
    bloodreaver: "The old mother! Legs everywhere, fangs like roasting spits, and a whole nursery in the walls. She bit me before I'd finished grinning. A fine first scar. I want another.",
    runeguard: "The Broodmother keeps a cave of omens: lit legs, ripening eggs, threads drawn pillar to pillar. Every blow she deals is written somewhere first; I simply had not yet learned the hand.",
    gorgemaw: "My hostess keeps a larder in silk: eggs, venom, guests wrapped for later. Everything bound and saved. I intend to eat through her pantry before she gets round to me.",
    oathbroker: "Her Majesty the Broodmother conducts her business through intermediaries: eggs, threads, venom. Every clause of her contract is hidden in silk. I have begun on the fine print. So noted.",
    ironpalm: "She speaks with many legs at once. A crowded sentence. I listened, and understood little of it. Breathe. Tomorrow I will understand more.",
    ranger: "Cave of pillars and silk. Bad sightlines, worse footing. It hunts the way we do, from the dark. Respect.",
    shadowblade: "The mark has eight eyes, which I found frankly excessive. Still, every one of them was on our knight. There is always a blind side. I found hers.",
    pyromancer: "Her Leggy Majesty lives in a cave hung with silk, and silk, I am delighted to report, burns! She was less delighted. I lost most of an eyebrow to her venom. Worth it.",
    thermist: "The spider. Cold count: pillars, silk, eggs, a queen. Then she moved and I ran hot all at once, everything in the walls ticking toward hatching. I need to stay cold for this one.",
    driftblade: "The eight-legs lives in a cave strung with silk that catches you mid-stride, which is a crime against anyone who likes to run, and I ran anyway, round and through and out again.",
    marionettist: "Our leading lady makes a grand entrance: legs, fangs, and a nursery waiting in the wings. The set is all pillars and silk. My Doll was terrified. (I was not. I was at the back.)",
    cleric: "The spider queen fights with poison, hunger and her own children. I counted every bite on our people tonight. Dawn keep us; this one will test my patience and my prayers.",
    grovekeeper: "The old spinner keeps her cave like hard country: eggs ripening in the walls, silk on every path, the ground itself uncertain. Weather to be learned. My lot came back. That's a start.",
    chronomancer: "Skitterra. I will have met her a great many times by the end of this, I suspect. Tonight she was a tangle of legs and timing, and I didn't yet know the tune.",
    alembist: "Specimen: a broodmother spider, venomous, damp cave. Ailment profile: poisons, bites, eggs, sticky floors. A long shelf of remedies wanted. Note: silk is less flammable than I'd hoped. Tested.",
    threadweaver: "Another seamstress, and a sloppy one: threads strung any old way, pillar to pillar, snagging anyone who walks through. My little stitches came home tangled. I'll be unpicking her work for weeks.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats her
  win: {
    bastion: "The Queen fell at the hub with the floor gone round us. Bad footing to the last, and we stood on her own silk to finish it. Line intact. Held.",
    bloodreaver: "The old mother's down! Every bite she gave me, I gave back with interest. My arms are more scab than skin. The finest scars I own. I'll miss her.",
    runeguard: "The Broodmother is dead, and every omen she wrote, we read in time at last. Her final fall I saw a full breath ahead. As foreseen; eventually.",
    gorgemaw: "My hostess, served at last. Tough in the legs, bitter in the venom, generous in the portions. I have had shorter meals and few finer. Still hungry.",
    oathbroker: "Her Majesty has defaulted on every obligation, and the undersigned have collected in full. Her estate is a ruined cave and some eggshell. Contract closed. Countersigned.",
    ironpalm: "She said everything she had to say. We answered every word. Then she was quiet. I breathed.",
    ranger: "Brought it down on the last strip of floor. Long hunt. Clean kill.",
    shadowblade: "The mark died at the heart of her own web, still glaring at our knight. Fitting. It never saw me, not once, not even at the end.",
    pyromancer: "She's down, the cave is scorched, and I am a legend! Both eyebrows growing back, one sleeve gone entirely. Mostly unsinged, and entirely magnificent.",
    thermist: "Down. Cold, clean, done. Then it all came rushing up hot and I laughed until my hands shook. Back to the middle, and stayed there.",
    driftblade: "We beat her and I ran one last lap of the cave through the torn silk for the joy of it, round every pillar left standing and out the far side, never landed once.",
    marionettist: "Curtain! Our leading lady takes her final bow at the hub and does not rise for an encore. My Doll is unharmed and insufferable about it. (Standing ovation.)",
    cleric: "The spider queen is gone, and every one of us walked out of the dark. I said a quiet prayer for her children too. Thank the light.",
    grovekeeper: "The old spinner's season is over. The cave went quiet like a field after harvest. My lot are tired and scratched and all still standing. A good year.",
    chronomancer: "Skitterra is dead. I'd say at last, but from where I sit she always was going to be. We only had to find the right bar of the song.",
    alembist: "Specimen deceased. Remedies used: all of them. Shelves bare, robe scorched, one explosion, entirely intended. Note: her venom makes a lovely base for something. Bottled.",
    threadweaver: "The competition is undone. Every thread she strung, we cut or walked. My little stitches held together through all of it. Pulled it tight, at last.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Venom Mist': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She breathed a green mist over the whole cave. Nothing to block. I drew the line in close to the healer before it rolled out, braced, and let it pass. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole cave choked on her breath! Barely a scratch on me, shameful, but the others feel it, so we come in close by the menders, all of us whole beforehand, and drink it down together.",
      runeguard: "The omen: her body swells and she exhales. The answer: everyone whole beforehand and gathered within reach of mending, for the mist spares no corner. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "A broth served to the whole table at once. Thin, bitter, faintly green. There is no refusing it, so we gathered by the healers, full beforehand, and swallowed it together. Underseasoned.",
      oathbroker: "A charge levied upon all the undersigned in common, at regular intervals. It cannot be avoided, only met solvent. We gathered by our healers, whole, and paid it. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke to all of us at once, in one long breath. We gathered by the menders and listened. It passed. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Mist filled the whole cave. No lane out of it. Came in close to the menders before it rolled and took it standing.",
      shadowblade: "The mark breathed on everyone, which was ill-mannered of it. Nowhere to slip away to, so I came in close to our menders and waited for the air to clear. Unpleasant, never dangerous.",
      pyromancer: "A green fog, everywhere at once, the colour of trouble! Nowhere to escape it, so I stood by the healers and cast straight through. The mist put my sleeve out, which I suppose was a kindness.",
      thermist: "Cold read: nowhere is safe, so be whole before it comes. Then the mist was on us, wet and hot in the throat, and I was already tucked in with the others. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The mist rolled through the whole cave and there was nowhere to run, which I hated, so I ran in close to the menders instead and let it blow through all of us at once.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady breathes upon the entire house. No exits, no wings. The company gathered downstage by the healers and took it as one. (The Doll coughed, theatrically.)",
      cleric: "The mist reaches every one of us, every time. I made sure nobody was still hurting from the last thing before she breathed, and laid the light over them all at once. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Her breath comes on like fog off a marsh, over every field at once. I planted on my lot while it was still gathering in her, so the mending was rooted when it touched them. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "She breathes on the whole cave, and later breathes again; it is the chorus of her song. I had everyone whole a beat before it, every time after the first. The first, we'll not discuss.",
      alembist: "Ailment: venom mist, whole party, no escape. Remedy: everyone topped up beforehand, the antidote brewed in the quiet before her breath. Note: it does not keep; dose it fresh each time.",
      threadweaver: "The mist reached every stitch at once. I drew the whole hem in close to the menders, so the threads could carry the hurt between us evenly. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Silk Wrap': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She swung the wrap at me with her forelegs, and the sweep in front of her reaches wide. Shield up in the last instant, line kept clear of her front. It pinned my feet after. Bad footing. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The old mother wrapped me in silk and bit straight through it. A good one! Then my feet stuck, so I made her come to me. The others kept to her sides, out of the sweep.",
      runeguard: "The omen: her forelegs draw back toward whoever holds her. The answer: a ward the breath before, and everyone else away from her front, for the sweep is broad. It binds the feet after; choose your ground first.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess served the wrap straight to me, as a proper hostess should: silk outside, a bite within. I opened wide just before, then stood stuck to my chair, chewing. Overcooked.",
      oathbroker: "The wrap is addressed to me as principal: a heavy blow, with a clause binding my feet afterward. I arranged my defences before signing, and the undersigned kept clear of her front. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "She wrapped me in silk, then bit. I raised my guard in its last instant. My feet would not move after, so I answered from where I stood. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Her front sweep went for our knight. Wide arc. Kept to her flank, well outside it. The knight stood planted after. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark flung its silk in our knight's face, as it always does. I was behind it, naturally, where the wrap never reaches. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "She lashed silk at our knight, wide as a fan in front of her! I'd planted myself behind her already, so I kept right on casting. The knight came out glued to the floor, glowing with indignation.",
      thermist: "Cold: the knight takes the wrap; the rest of us stay off her face. Hot: it hit him so hard the silk hissed, and he stood stuck while she moved on. I kept to her side.",
      driftblade: "She flung the wrap at our knight out front, so I went round through her side and out the back, well clear of the sweep, and he stood there stuck to the floor, poor thing.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady wraps the knight in silk, front and centre. The company keeps to the wings at her sides. (The knight is rooted to his mark; a fine piece of blocking.)",
      cleric: "The wrap came for our brave knight; it lands heavy, then holds him in place. I laid the light on him before it struck, and the rest of us kept away from her face. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The wrap falls on our shield like hail on a single field. I sowed on him before it came, kept my lot off her front, and he stood rooted after like a fencepost.",
      chronomancer: "The wrap comes for the one in front, then holds his feet a while. When it lands a beat before her legs begin striking, he must already stand where the first strikes won't fall. He will have; he did.",
      alembist: "Ailment: wrap and bite on our knight, heavy, with a binding after. Remedy: mending applied before it lands, everyone else off her front. Note: the binding wears off on its own; no reagent needed.",
      threadweaver: "The wrap is meant for our knight alone, so I kept every other stitch out of her front and ran a thread to him to share the bite. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Eightfold Lunge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Her legs lit up in order, and she struck each way and its opposite at once, turning as she went. I stepped onto the ground the last strike had just left and followed it round. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She stabbed both ways at once, turned, and stabbed again! Took one the first night. A good one. Now I watch the lit legs and step in behind each strike as it passes.",
      runeguard: "The omen: her legs light in sequence, and the sequence is the order of the strikes. The answer: each strike takes a direction and its opposite; step into the ground just struck and trail the turning. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess serves this course in pairs, from opposite ends of the table, turning like a lazy carousel. The trick is to sit where the last plate was just cleared. Sent nothing back.",
      oathbroker: "The terms are written on her legs, which light in the order she will strike, each blow paired with its opposite. The fine print: ground just struck is paid off. I followed the debt round. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Her legs spoke in order, a pair of words at once, back to back, turning. I stepped into the place she had just spoken. Then the next. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Legs light in order. Read them like tracks. It strikes both ways, turns, strikes again. Moved in behind each strike, onto ground it had finished with. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark struck in front and behind at once, then turned and did it again. So I stepped into the ground it had just struck; it never looks back at what it already hit. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Her legs lit up in order like a chandelier! She strikes both sides at once and turns. I hopped into each spot she'd just struck, right behind the turn, casting all the way. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: lit legs, the order, the turn. Each strike hits a way and its opposite. Step where it just landed. Hot: the ground hissed at my heels as I chased the turning round her. Close. Right.",
      driftblade: "She stabbed both ways and turned and stabbed again, and I followed right behind each strike into the ground it had just left, round and round her with the turning, never landing.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady performs a turning dance, striking upstage and downstage at once. Her lit legs are the cue sheet. The company stepped in behind each strike. (My Doll pirouetted.)",
      cleric: "Her legs light before she strikes, both directions at once, turning each time. I told our young archer twice to step in behind the last strike, not ahead of the next. Nobody fell. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Her legs light in order before she strikes, like a wind veering round the compass. Ground just struck is quiet ground. I kept stepping into it, behind the turn, and my lot came with me.",
      chronomancer: "A round in strikes: both ways at once, a turn, again. The ground she's just struck is safe for the rest of the verse. I stepped in behind each beat, which I'll have done before, surely.",
      alembist: "Ailment: strikes in opposed pairs, rotating. Remedy: read the lit legs, then step into whichever ground was struck last. Note: anyone standing ahead of the turn spoils quickly.",
      threadweaver: "She lunges both ways at once and turns between strikes. I walked my little stitches in behind each one, onto ground it had just finished with, nobody straying ahead. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Silk Lines': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She strung lines between pairs of us. A long line wanted its ends far apart; on the hard nights a short one wanted its pair side by side. Each pair kept its ground until the silk let go. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Silk between a pair of the others! The long one they pulled taut, far apart as the cave allows. The short ones stayed close as lovers. No scars for anyone. Disappointing, honestly.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a thread drawn between a pair of the party. The answer: a long thread is stretched until it sings, a short one kept close until it fades. Its colour tells which; the Broodmother is nothing if not consistent.",
      gorgemaw: "Silk strung between diners, like noodles. The long ones you stretch until they're thin as thread; the short ones you eat side by side. Al dente, when handled properly.",
      oathbroker: "A bond binding a pair of the undersigned. A long thread: stretched to its full term. A short one: kept close until discharged. The fine print is in the length. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "Silk between me and the mage. Long. We walked apart until it was taut, and waited. Short threads ask for closeness instead. I listened for which. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Got a line to the mage. Long one. Walked to opposite sides of the cave till it pulled taut. Short ones stay close. Easy if you read which.",
      shadowblade: "The mark tied me to our poor alchemist with a long thread. We drifted to opposite walls until it drew tight, and it was left holding nothing. Short ones, I'm told, prefer company.",
      pyromancer: "Tied by silk to the archer! A long line, so we stalked off to opposite ends of the cave, very dramatic. Short lines, I learned the hard way, want you cosy instead.",
      thermist: "Cold: a long line, far apart; a short line, together. Hold it until it lets go. Hot: I nearly ran back in too early. Too far is right, for once.",
      driftblade: "She tied me to the archer with a long thread and for once I got to run far and fast, clear across the cave until it hummed, and then wait there, still, which I hated.",
      marionettist: "A pair of the company are tied with silk. A long line: exits at opposite wings. A short line: a duet, close together, centre stage. (Hold the pose until the line fades.)",
      cleric: "She ties our people together with silk. A long thread wants them far apart; on harder nights a short one wants a pair nearly arm in arm. I kept close watch until each one faded.",
      grovekeeper: "Lines of silk between my lot, like runner beans on a string. A long one wants pulling taut, far apart; a short one wants tending close. Each pair kept to their own row.",
      chronomancer: "Silk between a pair of us, for a slow count. A long thread: far apart until it lets go. A short one: close until it lets go. Next time, which was last time, nobody guessed.",
      alembist: "Ailment: silk between a pair of us. Remedy, by label: long line, far apart; short line, close together; hold the dose until it fades. Note: do not mix up the labels.",
      threadweaver: "Her lines, between my little stitches. Long ones want tension: far apart, pulled tight. Short ones want no slack at all: side by side. At last, a seamstress who knows what she wants.",
    } },
    'Spin': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She strung threads pillar to pillar round the middle. Crossing one stings and pins the feet, so we walked round them and broke the knots by the pillars to cut them for good. Better footing. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Silk across the cave, pillar to pillar. Walked through one: a sting, then stuck. Barely a scratch, shameful. The knots by the pillars break, and a broken knot drops its thread. So I broke them.",
      runeguard: "The omen: silk drawn pillar to pillar, a square about the hub. The answer: never cross a thread; break its knot beside the pillar and it is cut for good. Each one left whole she will use later, in the dark.",
      gorgemaw: "Silk strung across the dining room, pillar to pillar. Walk through it and it stings. Each strand is tied off by a knot at its pillar; I bit through the knots and the strands fell. Chewy. No flavour at all.",
      oathbroker: "She has drawn threads pillar to pillar, which sting and detain any trespasser. Each is secured by a knot beside its pillar; break the knot and the thread is void. Every thread she keeps is a claim on us later. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Silk between the pillars. Walk through it, and it stings and holds. I did not walk through it. I broke its knot by the pillar instead. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Threads between the pillars. Square round the middle. Step through one and it stings and roots. Shot the knots at the pillar ends; each one drops a thread.",
      shadowblade: "The mark hung silk across the room like a burglar's bell. Step through and it stings and holds you. Each thread has a knot by its pillar; I slipped round the outside and opened them one by one.",
      pyromancer: "Silk strung pillar to pillar! I walked through one in a moment of artistic passion, and it stung and held me. Each thread is tied off with a knot by its pillar, and knots burn. Gloriously.",
      thermist: "Cold: threads in a square, pillar to pillar; don't cross; the knots by the pillars cut them. Hot: I crossed one in a rush and it stung and stuck me. Went back for the knot.",
      driftblade: "She strung threads right across my lanes, which is unforgivable, and walking through one stings and sticks you, so I broke the knots by the pillars and then ran through where they'd been.",
      marionettist: "The set is strung with silk, pillar to pillar, and the company must not walk through it; it stings and holds. Each strand is tied off by a pillar. (Strike the set: knots first.)",
      cleric: "Threads between the pillars sting and hold whoever walks through; our impatient rogue learned that twice. Break a knot by its pillar and that thread is gone. I asked them to, kindly, and they did.",
      grovekeeper: "She strung silk round the middle like a fence round a pasture. Walking through it stings and holds you. The knots by the pillars are its posts; pull those up and the fence comes down.",
      chronomancer: "Threads, pillar to pillar; they sting and hold whoever walks through. Their knots break, and every thread cut now is one drop fewer from her in the dark, later. Later, which I have seen.",
      alembist: "Ailment: threads pillar to pillar; crossing one stings and binds. Remedy: break the knot by its pillar, and the thread is cut for good. Note: each thread left whole is a spider dropping on us in the dark.",
      threadweaver: "A sloppy seam, pillar to pillar, snagging anyone who walks through. Each thread ends in a knot by its pillar; unpick the knot and the whole thread falls. I set my stitches to unpicking.",
    } },
    'Venom Spit': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She spat a narrow line at those of us farthest from her. They stepped aside as it came, and kept to the edge, so the venom pooled by the wall, away from our ground. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She spits at whoever keeps their distance. Never me; I'm in her face, where the biting is. The far ones sidestep at the very last and leave her venom out by the wall.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she turns her head toward whoever stands farthest. The answer: those at the edge step aside as it comes, and stay at the edge, so the pool it leaves lies out of everyone's way.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess spits at guests who sit far from the table. Rude, but fair. The far ones stepped aside at the last moment, and her spittle pooled by the wall. A sour sauce, wasted on the floor.",
      oathbroker: "A charge levied on those who keep their distance. The fine print: it leaves a residue that slows. The parties concerned step aside at the last moment and leave it at the wall. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke to the ones far away. A thin, sharp word. They stepped aside as it came, and it pooled by the wall, where no one walks. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Spits at the far ones. That's me. Narrow line. Stepped sideways at the last, out at the edge, and left the pool by the wall. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark spits at those who stand farthest, which has never once been me; I'm at its back. I watched our archer sidestep it neatly and leave the puddle out by the wall.",
      pyromancer: "She spat at me for standing at a sensible distance! A narrow green line. I stepped aside just as it came and left the slime pooling at the wall. My hem is ruined.",
      thermist: "Cold: the farthest ones, a narrow line, step aside late, keep the puddle at the wall. Hot: I dodged too early, the line followed me, and I had to jump again. Late is right.",
      driftblade: "She spat at me out at the edge, a thin green line, and I slipped sideways at the last moment, through the gap, and the venom pooled at the wall behind me where nobody needs to run.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady spits at the back row. The back row steps aside at the last moment and leaves the puddle in the wings. (The Doll was nowhere near. Of course.)",
      cleric: "She spits at whoever stands farthest away, and the venom pools where it lands. Those at the edge stepped aside as it came and kept it by the walls. Nobody lingered in it. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "She spits at the ones far out, a thin green line, and it leaves a bog behind. Those at the edge stepped aside late and kept the bog by the wall, where it spoils nobody's ground.",
      chronomancer: "A thin line at whoever's farthest. Her aim settles a beat before it lands; step aside on that beat, not before. The puddle stays, so it had best stay by the wall.",
      alembist: "Ailment: venom spat at whoever stands farthest, leaving a slowing pool. Remedy: stand far, stand by the wall, step aside late. Note: the pool spoils the floor, so keep it off our shelf.",
      threadweaver: "She spits at whichever stitch has wandered farthest. Those at the edge stepped aside late and kept the puddle out by the wall, well off our hem. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Egg Sacs': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights she drops egg sacs round the web, each wanting one of us on it. One soldier to each sac, feet planted; the goo holds you a moment after. Leave one and it bursts on everyone. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Egg sacs! One of us on each, stomp it flat. It splashes and grips your feet, a sticky little sting. Leave one alone and it bursts over everyone, and that's not even a good scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: sacs settle on the web, each within its own ring. The answer: one of us to each, standing inside; the stomp holds the feet a moment. An unattended sac bursts upon us all and fouls its ground.",
      gorgemaw: "Caviar! Egg sacs scattered on the web, one portion each. We each stepped onto our own; mine was chewy and clung to my boots. Leave one uneaten and it splatters the whole table.",
      oathbroker: "On the hard nights she lays sacs, each demanding the signature of exactly one of the undersigned. One per sac, feet held a moment after. An unsigned sac defaults upon us all. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "Sacs on the web. Each asked for one of us. I answered mine with my foot. It held me a moment. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Sacs on the web, apart from each other. One of us each. Took the near one, stood in it, stomped. Feet stuck a moment. None left whole.",
      shadowblade: "Eggs, laid in the open. Careless of the mark. One of us to each sac; I took the one behind her, obviously. The stomp held my feet a moment. Worth it.",
      pyromancer: "Egg sacs dropped round the web, one for each of us! I stomped mine flat, mid-spell, and got stuck in the goo. Leave one and it bursts over everyone, which would ruin my robe.",
      thermist: "Cold: sacs on the web; one each; step in; stomp. Hot: the goo held my feet and she was already winding up behind me. Leave none. Back to the middle after.",
      driftblade: "Egg sacs dropped and each wanted one of us standing still on it, the worst thing she could ask of me, so I ran to mine, stomped it, stuck a moment, and ran on.",
      marionettist: "Egg sacs appear, each with a spotlight for a single performer. One of the company to each mark; leave one empty and the whole house is splashed. (My Doll took a mark. Bravely.)",
      cleric: "On harder nights she lays sacs across the web. Each wants one of us standing on it, and whoever does is held a moment. Leave one, and it bursts over us all. Dawn keep our feet.",
      grovekeeper: "She sows egg sacs round the web like seed. Each wants one of us to tread it in; leave one and it bursts over the whole field and sours the ground. My lot took one apiece.",
      chronomancer: "Sacs, one to each of us. Step on yours before it bursts. Your feet stay put a beat after the stomp, so don't plan on moving straight away. I'll have learned that, I'm sure.",
      alembist: "Ailment: egg sacs, each needing one body. Remedy: one of us per sac, stomp, accept a brief binding. Note: an untended sac bursts over everyone and leaves a pool. Spoiled stock.",
      threadweaver: "Egg sacs dropped round the web; each wanted one stitch standing on it. I sent one to each, no doubling up, none left bare. They held fast a moment after stomping. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Strand Reel': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She shot a strand to a far pillar and hauled herself down it. Anyone on that line is hit and stuck. We stepped off it, and I followed her to the pillar to keep her face on me. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She threw a strand at a pillar and flew down it! I was in the way once. A good one. Now I step off the line and run after her, so she's never lonely at her pillar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a strand flung from her to a distant pillar. The answer: leave the line between them before she reels herself along it, then follow, for she hangs at that pillar a while.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess throws a strand to a far pillar and slurps herself along it like a noodle, flattening anyone in the way. Step aside, then follow her to her new seat.",
      oathbroker: "She files a strand to a distant pillar and travels upon it, collecting from anyone standing on the line. Step off before the filing is executed, then attend her at her new address.",
      ironpalm: "She threw a line to a pillar and went along it. I was not on the line. Then I walked to her and continued. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Strand from it to a far pillar. It reels itself along. Stepped out of that lane. It hung on the pillar after; the close ones went with it.",
      shadowblade: "The mark threw a strand to a pillar and flung itself down it. Anyone on the line was hit and held; I'd already stepped aside. Then I followed and found its back again at the pillar.",
      pyromancer: "She shot a strand to a pillar and zipped along it like a comet, flattening anyone in the way! I'd already stepped out of its path. She hung there after, so the close fighters chased her.",
      thermist: "Cold: a strand to a far pillar; get off the line. Hot: she shot down it right past me, and I was already running after her. She waits at the pillar. Go to her.",
      driftblade: "She flung a strand to a pillar and flew along it, so I slipped off the line and then chased her straight through after, round the pillar, and found her hanging there waiting.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady flies on a wire to a far pillar! Anyone beneath the wire is struck and held. The company clears the path, then the close fighters follow her across the stage.",
      cleric: "She casts a strand to a far pillar and pulls herself along it, striking and holding anyone in the way. Our people stepped off the line, and those who fight close went after her.",
      grovekeeper: "She cast a strand to a far pillar and hauled herself down it like a river in spate. Stand clear of the channel. She rests at the pillar after, so the close fighters went to her.",
      chronomancer: "A strand to a pillar, then she follows it. Leave the line before the pull; once she arrives she lingers a beat or two, so the near fighters must go to her. Every time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: she reels herself to a far pillar, striking and binding anyone on the line. Remedy: clear the line, then those who fight close follow her over. Note: she lingers there.",
      threadweaver: "A strand from her to a pillar, then she reels herself down it, catching any stitch left on the line. I kept my little stitches off it, and the close ones followed her across.",
    } },
    'Fang Rush': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights she charges whoever stands farthest. Her line follows them until just before. They stepped aside late, or put a pillar in her way. Then nobody walks the silk she drags. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She runs down the farthest one herself! Not me, sadly. They wait until the last moment and step out of her line, or hide behind a pillar and let the stone take the fangs.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she fixes on whoever stands farthest, and her line follows them until a breath before. The answer: step aside late, or set a pillar in her way; then avoid the sticky trail she leaves.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess rushes the guest seated farthest away. They step aside late, or sit behind a pillar, and she gets a mouthful of stone. Her trail stays sticky, like spilt syrup.",
      oathbroker: "A charge against whoever sits farthest. Her line follows the debtor until just before execution; step aside then, or interpose a pillar as surety. Her trail is a lien. Avoid it.",
      ironpalm: "She ran at the farthest. Her line followed them until just before. They stepped aside then. Her trail was sticky; I walked round it. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Charges the farthest. Usually me. Line tracks you to the last moment. Put a pillar between us; she hit stone. Don't walk her trail after.",
      shadowblade: "The mark charges whoever stands farthest. Rude. Its line follows them, then settles; that is when you step aside. A pillar in the way works too. The trail it drags is sticky. Avoid it.",
      pyromancer: "She charged at me! Straight across the web! I waited until her aim settled, then leapt aside, and she thundered past trailing sticky silk. Hair: intact. Pride: wounded.",
      thermist: "Cold: farthest one; wait for the aim to settle; step aside, or a pillar between. Hot: she went past so close I felt the wind of her. Then her trail, sticky. Keep off it.",
      driftblade: "She charged at me across the web and I waited, hating every still moment, until her line settled, then slipped aside and she ran straight through where I'd been and left sticky silk behind.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady rushes the back row! The back row waits for her aim to settle, then exits. A pillar onstage serves as well. (She left sticky silk all over the set.)",
      cleric: "She runs down whoever stands farthest away, and her line follows them until just before. Our archer slipped behind a pillar and let the stone take it. Then we kept off the silk she left. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "She charges the one farthest out, like a gale down a valley. Her line settles just before; step aside then, or put a pillar in its path. The silk trail stays sticky a while.",
      chronomancer: "A charge at the farthest. The aim tracks them until a beat before it lands; move on that beat, or let a pillar take it. Her trail lingers. I'll have waited for the beat, as usual.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a charge at the farthest. Remedy: step aside once the aim settles, or let a pillar absorb it. Note: the pillar is chipped for its trouble, and the trail she leaves is sticky.",
      threadweaver: "She charges the stitch that's wandered farthest. They wait for her line to settle, then step aside or slip behind a pillar. Mind the sticky silk she drags behind her.",
    } },
    'Brood': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The ripest eggs hatched, a spiderling on each side of the web. They must die together, or the survivor goes wild. We brought both low evenly and finished them in the same breath. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "A pair of little ones hatched across the web! Kill one and the other goes mad. So we bled them both down evenly and finished them together. Cute. Bite hard.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the ripest clutches swell. The answer: they hatch as a pair, and the pair must fall together, for one left alone turns savage. Better still, break the clutches before they ripen. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess serves her children in pairs, one at each end of the table. Eat one and the other takes offence. So we ate them together. Soft-shelled. Delicate.",
      oathbroker: "The ripest clutches hatch, an heir each, and their estates are joint. Settle one and the other defaults violently. Settle both at once. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A pair hatched, across the web. When one is silenced, the other screams. So we silenced them together. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Pair hatched on opposite sides. Split our shots, brought both down together. Kill one alone and the other goes wild. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark's children hatched in a pair. Kill one and the other takes it personally. So we ended them together, which is tidier anyway. They never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Eggs hatched! A pair of spiderlings, across the web from each other. Kill one and the other loses its temper. So we burned them down evenly, together. Also: break eggs early. Fewer spiders.",
      thermist: "Cold: a pair hatches, across the web; kill together. Hot: we killed one too fast and the other went mad. Fury. Back to the middle: wear them evenly, finish together.",
      driftblade: "A pair hatched across the web from each other and they hate dying alone, so we wore both down evenly, me running between them through the middle, and they died together.",
      marionettist: "A pair of spiderlings take the stage, one in each wing. Kill one and the other throws a tantrum. The company finished them together. (Better still: break the eggs before their entrance.)",
      cleric: "Her children hatched as a pair, and they do not like dying alone; when one falls, the other rages. We brought both low and ended them together. I prayed for them a little.",
      grovekeeper: "The ripest clutches hatched in a pair, across the web. Reap them together, or the one left standing runs wild. Better yet, break the clutches before they ripen.",
      chronomancer: "A pair hatches. Kill one, and the other has a few beats before it turns wild. So they fall on the same beat, or near it. The clutches can be broken beforehand, of course.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a pair of spiderlings, hatched across the web. Remedy: wear both down evenly, kill together; one alone turns savage. Note: a clutch broken early hatches nothing.",
      threadweaver: "A pair hatched across the web, tied to each other. Cut one and the other snaps wild. So we wore them down evenly and cut them both at once.",
    } },
    'Silk Reel': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She hooked whoever stood farthest from her and reeled them in. A quick bound away the moment it pulls snaps the thread; reaching her means a bite. I keep her ready, but nobody should arrive. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She reels in whoever's farthest, for a bite! Lucky them. They don't want it, though, so they pull away and then leap hard, and the thread snaps.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a thread fastens on whoever is farthest. The answer: one sharp leap away as the pull begins snaps it; struggling against it slows it. Arriving at her means a venomous bite.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess fishes for the guest farthest from the table and reels them in. They leap away to snap the line. Arrive, and she takes a bite. Rude, eating one's guests.",
      oathbroker: "A thread on whoever's farthest: a summons to her mouth. Pulling away delays it; a sharp leap quashes the summons outright. On harder nights, the summons also names our healer. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "She hooked the farthest and pulled. They pulled back, then leapt. The thread snapped. Breathe.",
      ranger: "She hooked me. Farthest away, of course. Pulled against it to slow it, then a quick leap, and the thread snapped.",
      shadowblade: "The mark hooked the farthest of us. Never me; I'm right behind it. Our archer leapt away as the thread pulled, and it snapped. Dragged all the way in, one gets bitten.",
      pyromancer: "She hooked me with silk and started reeling! I leapt away sharp as the pull began, and the thread snapped. Being dragged all the way in means a bite. Not glamorous.",
      thermist: "Cold: hooked, farthest; walk away to slow it, one leap to snap it. Hot: I froze, she dragged, I panicked. Leapt. Snapped. Too close.",
      driftblade: "She hooked me from across the cave and began dragging, so I pulled against it and then leapt away through the air and the thread snapped clean, and I was gone.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady fishes for the back row with a line of silk. The hooked performer pulls against it, then leaps away, and the line snaps. (Dragged onstage means bitten.)",
      cleric: "She hooks whoever stands farthest and reels them in for a bite; on harder nights, she hooks me as well. A quick leap away snaps the thread. If the bite lands anyway, I mend it.",
      grovekeeper: "A thread caught me out at the edge and began to reel. Pull against it to slow it; one sharp bound away and it snaps, like a weed's root. Reaching her ends in a bite.",
      chronomancer: "A thread on whoever's farthest; she pulls after a beat. Leap on that beat and it snaps. Reaching her is a bite. On the hard nights she hooks me too, which is personal.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a thread on whoever's farthest, reeling them in for a venomous bite. Remedy: pull away to slow it, one sharp leap to snap it. Note: on harder nights, it takes me too.",
      threadweaver: "She hooks the stitch farthest out and reels them in. Pull against it, then one sharp leap, and it snaps. Arriving at her ends in a bite. On harder nights, she hooks me. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Acid Deluge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She went to the middle, reared up and sprayed the whole cave. Only a pillar's shadow is safe, so the line got behind one together. Each pillar that shelters someone takes a crack. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Acid everywhere! I wanted to taste it. The others dragged me behind a pillar, out of her line from the middle. Every pillar that hides someone cracks, so we shared one.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she scuttles to the hub and rears. The answer: a pillar between yourself and her before the acid falls. Every pillar used is chipped, and enough uses bring it down; share them sparingly.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess douses the whole dining room in vinegar from the middle. A pillar between you and her keeps you dry. Each pillar used takes a splash, so we crowded behind one. Far too acidic.",
      oathbroker: "A levy upon all who stand in her sight from the middle. A pillar between oneself and her is exemption. Each pillar used pays a share, and enough shares bring it down. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "She went to the middle and spoke acid in every direction. We stood behind a pillar, where her voice did not reach. Breathe.",
      ranger: "It went to the middle and reared. Got a pillar between us. Acid hit everything but the shadow behind it.",
      shadowblade: "The mark goes to the middle and sprays the whole room. I took shelter behind a pillar, out of its sight. Each pillar used is chipped, and they don't last forever. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "She sprayed acid over the whole cave! I hid behind a pillar, shoulder to shoulder with the others, out of her sight from the middle. Each sheltering pillar cracks a little. My sleeve dissolved.",
      thermist: "Cold: she's central; a pillar between us; share the shelter. Hot: acid on every side, roaring. The pillar cracked. On the hard nights the floor stays sticky after. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "She went to the middle and sprayed the whole cave and I ran behind a pillar with the others, the only dry ground, and waited there, still, which I hated, while the stone cracked.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady takes centre stage and sprays the whole house. The company hides behind the scenery, out of her sightline. Each pillar used is chipped. (The set took a beating.)",
      cleric: "She sprays acid over the whole cave from the middle; only a pillar's shadow is safe. We gathered behind one together, and I mended whoever was splashed. Thank the light for stone.",
      grovekeeper: "Acid came down from the middle like a squall. Only a pillar's lee was dry. My lot gathered behind one; each sheltering pillar is weathered by it, so we shared.",
      chronomancer: "She moves to the middle and rears; a beat later, acid everywhere but behind the pillars. Each pillar used cracks, so we shared one, which I'll have suggested.",
      alembist: "Ailment: acid over the whole cave, from the middle. Remedy: a pillar between you and her. Note: each sheltering pillar chips. On harder nights the floor stays sticky. Spoiled.",
      threadweaver: "Acid from the middle, everywhere but behind the pillars. I gathered the whole hem behind one, close. Each pillar used is chipped. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Into the Dark': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She climbed into the dark, out of reach, and the lights failed. No face to hold. So I put my weight on the knots of her threads and the eggs until she came down. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She climbed into the dark! Gone! Rude. Nobody to bleed on, so I broke the knots on her threads and smashed eggs until she came back down.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she climbs, and the lights fail. The answer: break the knots on her threads, in the order she will run them, and the eggs, before she drops from the dark.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess left the table and climbed into the dark. While she was gone we broke the knots on her threads and ate her eggs. Rude, leaving guests unattended.",
      oathbroker: "She has retired into the dark. In her absence, the undersigned break the knots of her threads, in the order she means to use them, and settle the eggs. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "She went into the dark, silent. Nobody to answer. So we broke the knots and the eggs. Breathe.",
      ranger: "It went up into the dark. Lost sight. Shot the knots on its threads, then the eggs, while it hunted overhead.",
      shadowblade: "The mark vanished into the dark, which is my trick, thank you. While it hunted overhead, I broke the knots on its threads and then the eggs.",
      pyromancer: "She climbed into the dark and the lights went out! So I lit the place myself, burning the knots on her threads, then the eggs, until she came back.",
      thermist: "Cold: she's gone; knots first, then eggs. Hot: the dark was full of her, overhead, running. I kept working. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "She climbed into the dark and I ran through the pools of light, breaking knots on her threads and smashing eggs, never landing, never stopping.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady exits into the flies, and the house lights dim. The company strikes the set: knots on her threads, then eggs. (The Doll found a spotlight.)",
      cleric: "She climbed into the dark and the cave dimmed to pools of light. Our people broke the knots on her threads and then the eggs, and I kept them in the light. Dawn keep us in the dark.",
      grovekeeper: "She went up into the dark like the sun before a storm. A quiet season, and not to be wasted: we broke the knots on her threads, then the clutches. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "She climbs into the dark. A long interval. We break the knots on her threads in the order she'll run them, then the eggs. I'll have broken them sooner this time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: she climbs into the dark, out of reach. Remedy: use the quiet to break the knots on her threads, then the eggs. Note: in order; she runs them in turn.",
      threadweaver: "She climbed into the dark and left her threads behind. We unpicked the knots on them, in the order she'd use them, then the eggs. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'From the Dark': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Her shadow ran overhead along a thread, and she dropped at its far end. We left that ground. A thread already cut, she cannot use, so she hunts: the one who stands farthest from her gets the drop, and it follows them until the last moment. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She ran along a thread overhead and dropped at its far end! I stepped out of the landing, then hit her. Cut a thread and she hunts the farthest of us instead, and the circle tracks you. On the hard nights she drops twice.",
      runeguard: "The omen: her shadow runs along an uncut thread. The answer: leave the circle at its far end. A thread already cut, she hunts the one farthest from her, her circle following until a breath before it lands; on harder nights, twice in turn.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess scurries along a thread overhead and drops onto its far end like a dish from a height. We left that seat. A thread cut beforehand, she can't use, so she goes after whichever guest sits farthest away, and the plate follows them.",
      oathbroker: "She travels along any uncut thread and descends at its far end. The undersigned vacate that ground. A thread voided beforehand is replaced by a pursuit of whoever stands farthest, the circle tracking them until the last moment. On harder nights, twice. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Her shadow ran along a thread. She fell at its end. I was not there. A cut thread she cannot use, so she came for the farthest of us instead. It followed. I kept walking. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Shadow ran down a thread. Read where it ended. Left the circle. Cut thread: she hunts the one farthest out, circle tracks them till just before. Hard: twice.",
      shadowblade: "The mark's shadow ran along a thread and dropped at its far end. I knew where it was going before it did. Cut the thread and it hunts the farthest of us instead, the circle sticking to its mark until the very end. Keep moving.",
      pyromancer: "Her shadow ran overhead along a thread and she dropped at its end! I left the circle there, mid-spell. A thread cut beforehand and she hunts the farthest of us instead, and the circle follows you about like a puppy. Twice, on the hard nights.",
      thermist: "Cold: shadow along a thread; she drops at its far end; leave. Cut thread: she hunts whoever stands farthest, the circle tracking until a breath before. Hot: that was me, and it followed. Keep moving.",
      driftblade: "Her shadow ran along a thread overhead and I ran the other way, out of the circle at its far end. A cut thread she can't use, so she hunted whoever was farthest, and the circle chased them until the last moment. I kept running.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady runs along a thread in the flies and drops onto its far end. The company clears that mark. A thread already cut means a different entrance: she hunts whoever stands farthest, the spotlight following them. (Twice on harder nights.)",
      cleric: "Her shadow runs along a thread and she drops where it ends. We left that ground. Where a thread was cut, she hunts the one farthest from her instead, and the circle follows them until the last moment. On harder nights she drops twice. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Her shadow ran along a thread like a cloud over a field. Where it ended, she fell like hail. A thread cut beforehand, she hunts the farthest of my lot instead, the circle following them close until the last moment.",
      chronomancer: "Her shadow runs along a thread, then she drops at its end. Cut the thread and she hunts the farthest of us instead, the circle following until a beat before; on harder nights, twice in turn. I'll have kept moving.",
      alembist: "Ailment: she drops at the far end of an uncut thread. Remedy: leave the circle, or break that thread's knot first. Note: a cut thread makes her hunt the farthest of us instead, the circle tracking until a moment before it lands. Twice on harder nights.",
      threadweaver: "She runs along any thread nobody cut, and drops at its far end. I moved my stitches off that ground. A cut thread, and she hunts the one stitch farthest from her instead, the circle following until the last moment. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Cocoon Drop': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cocoon fell on one of the young ones, never me, and the dead weight of it wanted us all. Everyone in, all but one at least; on the hard nights, every last one of us. The line closed on whoever it was. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "A cocoon dropped on one of the others and the rest of us had to pile in! Together or not at all. Normal nights she'll forgive one straggler. Hard nights, no one. Closer, then. Closer!",
      runeguard: "The omen: a cocoon gathers over one of the party, never the knight. The answer: all gather on them, all but one on the ordinary nights, every one of us on the harder nights. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A cocoon, swung down onto one guest, and the rest of us were expected at that table. All but one on a gentle night; every seat taken on a hard one. A thin portion if you're alone.",
      oathbroker: "A cocoon descends upon one of the undersigned, never the shield. Terms: the others attend, all but one on the ordinary terms, all of us on the hard. The fine print: absentees raise the charge for everyone present. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A cocoon fell on one of us. The rest went to them. On the harder nights, all of us. I went in. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Cocoon over one of the others, not the tank. Everybody in on them. Hard nights, everybody, no exceptions. Closed the gap.",
      shadowblade: "A cocoon dropped on one of the others, and I was invited. Everyone has to be in, or nearly. On the hard nights, everyone. I went in, quickly, and out again quicker.",
      pyromancer: "A cocoon fell on one of us, not the knight, and everyone had to run to share it! All but one, usually. All of us on the hard nights. I dashed in and I dashed out. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: cocoon over someone, not the shield. All in. All but one is enough on an ordinary night, and none spare on a hard one. Hot: the one who lagged took it worse. Everyone in.",
      driftblade: "A cocoon dropped on one of us and the rest of us had to run to them, every one, or all but one. I was already moving. Then straight out, before the next drop.",
      marionettist: "A cocoon descends on one player, never the lead. The company gathers on their mark, all but one in an easy house; all of us in a hard one. (The Doll rode in on my shoulder.)",
      cleric: "A cocoon fell on one of my friends, never our knight, and the rest of us went to share it. All but one will do on an ordinary night, and every one of us on a hard one. I mended them after.",
      grovekeeper: "A cocoon dropped on one of my lot like a seed pod, and the rest drew in around it. All but one on an easy night, all of us on a hard one. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "A cocoon over one of the party, never the shield. All gather on it; on an ordinary night one may be missing, on a harder night none. I'll have been there already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a cocoon over one of us, never the knight. Remedy: everyone to them, all but one on the ordinary nights, all of us on the harder. Note: a single absentee costs the rest of us dear.",
      threadweaver: "A cocoon dropped over one of my little stitches, never the knight. I gathered the rest in around it, all but one, or all of them on the harder nights. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Venom Drip': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Venom gathered over each of our heads and followed us. Every one of us needed our own patch of floor. The line opened out, kept its distance until it fell, and closed again after. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Venom over every head! It burns you and anyone close by. I took mine alone, well away from the others, a clean little scald. Then straight back in.",
      runeguard: "The omen: venom gathers over every head at once, and it follows. The answer: each to their own patch of floor, well apart, until it falls; then the party closes again.",
      gorgemaw: "A drip of venom over every guest's head. One portion each, to be taken alone; share a plate and you get both helpings. We each retired to our own corner. A sour little amuse-bouche.",
      oathbroker: "A charge upon each of the undersigned severally, not jointly. Stand near another and you pay their share as well. We parted, paid, and reconvened. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Venom over every head. Each of us alone with it. We stepped apart and waited. It fell. Then we came together. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Venom over each of us. Fanned out like a hunting party, each to our own ground. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Venom over everyone's head, which was impolite. We each slipped off to our own patch of floor, and mine was, as ever, in the dark behind the mark. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Venom gathered over all our heads! I swept off to my own little stage, well away from everyone, and let it drip on me alone. Mostly unsinged; somewhat dissolved.",
      thermist: "Cold: venom over each head; step apart; your own ground. Hot: I drifted near the archer and we both scalded. Too close. Apart, then back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Venom gathered over every head, so we each ran off to our own patch, me the farthest, through the gap and out, and I waited there for the drip and came straight back.",
      marionettist: "Venom over every head in the company. Each performer takes a solo spot, well apart. (A rare scene where nobody shares the stage, least of all the Doll.)",
      cleric: "Venom gathered over each of our heads. Everyone needed their own patch of floor, apart, so nobody was struck twice. I mended them one by one after. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Venom over every head, like rain from separate clouds. My lot each found their own patch of ground, well apart, so no cloud rained on two. Then we drew in again.",
      chronomancer: "Venom over every head; it falls on a slow count, and it follows. We each took our own patch, apart, and drifted back together on the beat after. I'll have known.",
      alembist: "Ailment: venom over every head at once. Remedy: everyone on their own patch of floor, apart, and the mending thrown into the middle after. Note: let no two doses mix.",
      threadweaver: "Venom over each stitch's head. I cut the threads and sent them apart, each to their own patch, then pulled the whole hem back together after. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Tightening Web': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She came back down into the middle, and the blow of her landing reached every one of us. After that the outer web went sticky for good. The line fights inside it now. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She dropped back into the middle and shook the whole cave! Felt it in my teeth. Lovely. Then the outer web went sticky, so I keep the old mother well away from the rim.",
      runeguard: "The omen: her shadow runs back to the hub. The answer: be whole when she lands, for the blow reaches everyone; then keep inward, for the outer web stays sticky to the end.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess returns to the table with a thump that rattles every plate in the cave. Then she glazes the edges of the room in syrup. We dine in the middle from now on.",
      oathbroker: "She returns to the middle and levies a charge on all of us upon landing. A further clause: the outer web stays sticky for the remainder of the term. We conduct our business inside it.",
      ironpalm: "She came back to the middle and spoke once, to all of us. We were ready. Then the edges grew sticky, so we stayed near the middle. Breathe.",
      ranger: "It came back to the middle. Landing hit everyone. Outer web sticky after. Lost my long lanes. Kept inside the rim.",
      shadowblade: "The mark came back to the middle with a thump that reached everyone. After that the outer web turned sticky, so I keep to the inner floor now, and to its back.",
      pyromancer: "She landed in the middle and shook every one of us! Then the outer web turned sticky, for good. I've kept inside the rim ever since, which ruins all my best long-distance angles.",
      thermist: "Cold: she lands in the middle; everyone takes it; be whole first. Then the outer web stays sticky. Hot: I got caught on the rim and nearly boiled over. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "She came back to the middle and the landing shook us all, and then the outer web went sticky, which ruins half my runs, so I run the inner circle now, round and through.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady returns to centre stage with a crash the whole house feels. Then the outer stage turns to glue. (The company keeps to the inner boards for the rest of the run.)",
      cleric: "She came back down into the middle, and the landing reached every one of us; I had them whole before it. After that the outer web was sticky, so we kept inside it.",
      grovekeeper: "She came down into the middle like the first storm of autumn, felt in every field. I planted before it. After, the outer web turned sticky as a bog, and we kept to the inner ground.",
      chronomancer: "Her shadow runs home to the middle, and the landing reaches everyone; be whole a beat before. Afterward the outer web is sticky for the rest of the song. I'll have stayed inside it.",
      alembist: "Ailment: her landing in the middle, felt by all; then the outer web turns sticky for good. Remedy: everyone topped up before she lands, and inside the rim after. Note: the rim is spoiled.",
      threadweaver: "She came back to the middle and the landing tugged on every stitch. After that the outer web went sticky, so I keep the whole hem gathered inside it. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Spinneret Volley': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights she rained silk on everyone but me, a glob at a time, each following its target until just before it fell. They kept moving, apart from each other. I kept her still. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Silk globs falling on everyone but me! Insulting. The others keep walking, apart, and each glob chases its owner, settles a moment before it drops, and lands on empty floor.",
      runeguard: "The omen: silk gathers over all but whoever holds her. The answer: keep walking, apart, for each glob follows its mark until a breath before it falls, and they come in a long train.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess sprinkles silk on every guest but me, glob after glob, like a cook seasoning plates. They walk on and leave each glob on an empty seat. I was not served. Offended.",
      oathbroker: "A series of instalments against all but myself, each following its debtor until just before it falls due. The undersigned keep walking, apart, and each lands where they were. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Silk fell on the others, again and again. Each followed until just before. They walked steadily. Each landed behind them. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Silk on all of us but the knight. Each glob tracks you, then stops just before. Kept walking, steady, wide of the others.",
      shadowblade: "The mark rained silk on everyone but our knight, glob after glob, each following until just before it fell. I strolled on, apart, and left them landing in my wake. Terribly dull of it.",
      pyromancer: "Silk raining on me, glob after glob, each one tracking me until just before! I kept walking steadily, apart from the others, casting what I could on the move. My hair is mostly silk now.",
      thermist: "Cold: globs on all but the knight; each follows, then settles; keep walking, apart. Hot: I stopped to cast once and was glued to the floor. Keep moving.",
      driftblade: "Silk fell on us one glob after another, each chasing me until just before it dropped, so I ran a long easy circle, wide of the others, and every one landed behind me.",
      marionettist: "Silk rains on the company, glob after glob, each following its performer until just before. The company keeps walking, well apart. (The knight, untouched, looked bored.)",
      cleric: "On harder nights silk rains on all of us but our knight, glob after glob, each following until just before. We kept walking steadily, apart, and I mended whoever was caught.",
      grovekeeper: "Silk fell on us like sleet, each glob following one of us until just before it landed. My lot kept walking, steady and apart, and the sleet fell behind them.",
      chronomancer: "Globs fall on all but the knight, in a steady march. Each follows until a beat before it lands. Keep walking, apart, in tempo, and they fall behind you.",
      alembist: "Ailment: silk globs on all but our knight, each tracking its target until just before. Remedy: keep walking steadily, apart. Note: stand still and you are glued to the bench.",
      threadweaver: "Silk fell on every stitch but the knight, glob after glob, each following until just before. We kept walking, apart, the thread let out long. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Molt': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights she shed her skin, and the husk struck her last pattern of legs over again while she began a new one elsewhere. I kept the last pattern in mind and watched both. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She left her old skin behind and it fought on without her! The husk repeats her last strikes while she starts fresh ones. Twice the old mother! A good night.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she sheds her skin and leaves the husk standing. The answer: the husk repeats her previous strikes from where it stands while she begins anew; remember the last order, read the new.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess left her shell on the table and it served the last course again, from where it lay, while she began a new one. Reheated leftovers. I watched both plates.",
      oathbroker: "She sheds her skin, and the husk executes her previous terms again while she begins fresh ones. One must recall the last contract while reading the new. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "She shed her skin. The skin repeated her last words while she spoke new ones. I listened to both. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Shed her skin. Husk replays her last strikes from where it stands. She starts new ones. Watch both.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the mark sheds its skin, and the husk repeats its last strikes while the mark begins new ones. Twice the eyes, and still neither looking for me.",
      pyromancer: "She shed her skin! The husk repeated her last strikes while she started fresh ones. I kept the old pattern in my head and found the gaps in both. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: the husk replays her last strikes; she starts new ones; read both. Hot: legs on every side, I couldn't breathe. Remembered the last pattern. Found the gap.",
      driftblade: "She shed her skin and the husk went through her last strikes again while she started new ones, so I ran the gaps of both at once, through and round, never landing.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady sheds her costume and leaves it onstage, where it repeats her last scene while she begins the next. (The company must watch both performances.)",
      cleric: "On harder nights she sheds her skin, and the husk strikes as she struck last while she begins afresh. We remembered the last pattern and watched both. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "She shed her skin like a snake in spring, and the husk repeated her last strikes while she started new ones. My lot remembered the old pattern and watched both.",
      chronomancer: "She leaves her husk behind, and it repeats her last strikes while she plays new ones: the same tune sung as a round. Remember the last pattern; it comes again.",
      alembist: "Ailment: on harder nights her husk repeats her last strikes while she begins new ones. Remedy: remember the last pattern, read the new. Note: keep a label on each.",
      threadweaver: "She left her husk behind and it stitched her last strikes over again while she began new ones. I kept the old pattern in my head and watched both. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Ceiling Ambush': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights she climbed into the ceiling, and her shadow crossed to where one of us had stood. Her landing was marked on the floor. We left it; anyone inside is thrown into the walls. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Up into the ceiling she went, and her shadow ran to where one of us stood. I stayed in the circle once. Thrown into the wall! A good one. Now I step out.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she climbs into the ceiling, and her shadow crosses toward where one of us stood. The answer: leave the circle marked where she will land, before she drops and throws us to the walls.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess climbed into the rafters and dropped onto a guest's empty chair. Her landing is marked; we left it. Those who lingered were thrown into the walls like spoiled crockery.",
      oathbroker: "She ascends, and her shadow travels to where one of the undersigned stood at that moment. The landing is marked; vacate it, or be thrown into the walls. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "She climbed into the ceiling. Her shadow crossed to where one of us stood. We left that ground. She fell on nothing. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Up into the ceiling. Shadow crossed to where one of us had stood. Landing marked on the floor. Got out of it.",
      shadowblade: "The mark climbs into the ceiling and drops where one of us stood. Its landing is marked on the floor, which rather spoils the surprise. I stepped out of it. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "She vanished into the ceiling, and her shadow crossed to where I'd stood! The landing glowed on the floor. I left it, and she crashed down where I'd been.",
      thermist: "Cold: she climbs; her shadow runs to where one of us stood; landing marked; leave it. Hot: I was thrown into the wall once. Not again.",
      driftblade: "She climbed into the ceiling and her shadow raced across the floor to where I'd stood, so I ran out of the landing and through, and she dropped on nothing.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady vanishes into the flies, and her shadow crosses to where one of the company stood. The landing is marked. (The company exits. Nobody is thrown into the scenery.)",
      cleric: "On harder nights she climbs into the ceiling and drops where one of us stood. Her landing is marked on the floor; anyone still in it is thrown into the walls. We left it.",
      grovekeeper: "She climbed into the ceiling like a hawk going up, and her shadow crossed to where one of us stood. The landing was marked. We moved before she stooped.",
      chronomancer: "She climbs; her shadow runs to where one of us stood when she went up. The landing is marked. Leave it before she drops. I'll have been elsewhere.",
      alembist: "Ailment: on harder nights she drops from the ceiling where one of us stood. Remedy: leave the landing marked on the floor. Note: whoever stays is thrown into the walls.",
      threadweaver: "She climbed into the ceiling and her shadow crossed to where one stitch had stood. The landing was marked. We moved before she dropped. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'The Swarm': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Every clutch still standing hatched at once. They come in pairs and must die together. I gathered them on me, and we brought each pair down side by side. The eggs we broke earlier sent nothing. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Every egg left in the web hatched at once! Spiderlings everywhere, all teeth! Pairs again; kill one and its twin goes mad. We took them pair by pair. Fine scars all round.",
      runeguard: "The omen: every clutch left standing ripens together. The answer: they hatch in pairs, and each pair must fall together. Fewer clutches spared before means fewer spiderlings now. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess serves the whole nursery at once: every clutch left on the shelf hatches. Pairs again, to be eaten together. A buffet. The eggs we cracked earlier made it a smaller one, which the others insist is the point.",
      oathbroker: "Every clutch still standing hatches at once, each heir bound to another. Each pair is settled together, or the survivor defaults. Clutches broken earlier are discharged. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Every egg left hatched. They came in pairs. We silenced each pair together. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Every clutch left hatched at once. Pairs. Brought each pair down together. Fewer eggs before, fewer now.",
      shadowblade: "Every egg the mark had left hatched at once. Pairs, and they hate dying alone. We ended each pair together. Fewer eggs, fewer guests.",
      pyromancer: "Every clutch still standing hatched at once! Spiders everywhere! Pairs, again; we burned each pair down together. Broken eggs before meant fewer spiders now. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: every clutch left hatches; pairs; each pair down together. Hot: so many, so fast. Broke more eggs the next time. Fewer now. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Every egg left in the web hatched at once and I ran through them all, round and through, pair by pair, wearing each pair down together.",
      marionettist: "The whole chorus of spiderlings takes the stage at once! Pairs, as ever, and each pair must exit together. (Fewer eggs earlier means a smaller chorus.)",
      cleric: "Every clutch still standing hatched at once, and the cave filled with her children. We ended each pair together. Fewer eggs before meant fewer bites now. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Every clutch left standing hatched at once, a whole season's growth in one morning. We reaped each pair together. The fewer we left, the lighter the harvest.",
      chronomancer: "Every clutch still standing hatches at once. Pairs, each on the same beat. The fewer we left earlier, the shorter this verse. I'll have broken more.",
      alembist: "Ailment: every clutch left standing hatches at once. Remedy: each pair down together. Note: clutches broken earlier hatch nothing. Fewer jars on the shelf, fewer spiders.",
      threadweaver: "Every clutch still standing hatched at once, tied in pairs. We cut each pair together. Fewer eggs earlier meant fewer threads to cut now. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Chrysalis': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She wrapped herself in silk at the hub, and it closes slowly. No blow to brace for, so I struck it with the rest of the line, everything we had, before it shut. It tore. She came out staggered and open. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "She hid in a cocoon! Coward. I tore at it with every wound I'd saved up, all of us did, before it closed. She tumbled out dazed and soft. Then the real bleeding started.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she binds herself in silk at the hub. The answer: the whole party's force upon it before it closes; torn open, she staggers and lies exposed. Let it close, and she devours us all.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess wrapped herself up like a parcel left for later. We tore into the wrapping with everything before it sealed, and she fell out stunned and tender. Best course of the night.",
      oathbroker: "She seals herself in silk at the hub; the debt falls due when it closes, and the penalty is everything. The undersigned paid in full beforehand, tearing it open. Torn, she staggers and pays heavily. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "She wrapped herself in silk and fell silent. Silence is a question too. We answered with everything we had, all at once. It tore. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Cocooned at the hub. Every arrow into it before it closed. It tore. She fell out staggered, wide open.",
      shadowblade: "The mark sealed itself in silk at the hub. Hiding is my trade, darling, and it was doing it badly. We tore it open with everything before it closed; it came out staggered and soft.",
      pyromancer: "She wrapped herself in silk, near me, which is frankly an invitation! Everything I had, all of us, before it closed. It burst, and she fell out staggered. Eyebrows: gone again. Worth it.",
      thermist: "Cold: cocoon at the hub; it closes; tear it first. Hot: ran scalding, poured all of it in, the silk blackening. It tore. She staggered, and I poured in more.",
      driftblade: "She wrapped herself in silk at the hub and for once I stood still and struck, everything I had, every blade we owned, and it tore and she tumbled out dazed.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady retires behind silk for a costume change. The company storms the dressing room with everything before the door shuts. (She emerges staggered and exposed. Applause.)",
      cleric: "She sealed herself in silk at the hub, and venom dripped on us as it closed. I kept everyone on their feet so they could give it everything. It tore in time, and she fell out staggered. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "She wrapped herself at the hub like a seed in its husk, waiting for her season. We tore the husk open with everything before it ripened. She fell out staggered. A good reaping.",
      chronomancer: "She wraps herself at the hub, and it closes on a slow count. Everything into it before the last beat. Torn, she staggers. Let it close and nobody is undone, not even by me.",
      alembist: "Ailment: she cocoons at the hub; once closed, it is fatal. Remedy: every hand on it before it closes, the drips mended on the side. Note: torn open, she staggers and every blow bites deeper.",
      threadweaver: "She stitched herself into a cocoon at the hub, neat work for once. We unpicked it with everything we had before the last seam closed. She fell out staggered. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'The Web Gives Way': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The floor gave way ring by ring from the walls inward, each ring shuddering first. Worst footing I've known. We moved inside, onto the hub, the spokes, or silk we never cut. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The floor fell away! I was on a falling ring once: dropped, hurt, held, hauled back up the silk. A good one. Now I watch for the shudder and move in onto the spokes first.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a ring of floor shudders before it falls. The answer: step inward before it drops, onto the hub, a spoke, or a thread left uncut. Every thread we cut earlier is one less path now.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess pulled the tablecloth from under us, ring by ring, from the walls inward. Only the centre of the table, the spokes and the silk we didn't eat stayed put. We kept our seats there.",
      oathbroker: "The floor is repossessed ring by ring, from the walls inward, each ring served notice by a shudder. Retained property: the hub, the spokes, and any thread left uncut. We kept to it. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The floor trembled, then fell, ring by ring. It asked us to move. We moved inward, onto what stayed. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Ring shakes, then drops. From the walls in. Kept to the hub, the spokes, and the threads we'd left. Narrow footing. Still a line.",
      shadowblade: "The floor shudders, then falls away from the walls, ring by ring. Inward, then, to the hub and the spokes. Even standing on a strip of silk, I found its back. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The floor started dropping away, ring by ring! I hate moving, but I hate falling more, so at the first shudder I swept inside onto a spoke and cast from there. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: a ring shudders; it falls; the hub, spokes and uncut silk stay. Hot: I was on the falling ring once, lurching, held, hauled up. Back to the middle. Literally.",
      driftblade: "The floor fell away from the walls inward, ring by ring, so I ran inside along the spokes and through, round and along the silk, never once landing on anything that would fall.",
      marionettist: "The stage collapses ring by ring, from the wings inward; each ring trembles first, its cue. The company moves to the hub, the spokes, and the silk we spared. (The Doll is light; it barely noticed.)",
      cleric: "The floor fell ring by ring from the walls, each ring shuddering first. Our dear knight was slow once and fell, and I mended him as he was hauled up. Now we move inward at the first tremble.",
      grovekeeper: "The ground went from the walls inward like a riverbank in flood, a shudder before each slip. My lot stepped onto the hub, the spokes and the silk we'd left standing. The threads we left uncut were bridges.",
      chronomancer: "The floor shakes, then falls, ring by ring from the walls in. Step inside on the shake, not after the fall. The hub, the spokes and the uncut silk remain. I'll have been there already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the floor falls ring by ring from the walls, after a shudder. Remedy: move inward onto the hub, a spoke, or uncut silk. Note: those who fall are hurt and hauled back up; keep the mending ready.",
      threadweaver: "The floor fell ring by ring from the walls. I gathered my little stitches inside, on the hub, the spokes, and the threads we'd left whole. Her silk, holding us up. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Snare Weave': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "She went to her hub and wove snares over every inch, twice. Three clear pockets each time: I held the one beside her, then stepped round to the next. The two with eggs dropped them where they had stood. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Snares everywhere, twice over! Only three holes were clear, so I kept my friend company in the one by her and stepped round with her. The egg-carriers dropped their mess in the hole they'd left. No scar at all. Shameful.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she takes the hub and the web fills with snares, the second layer showing early, its pockets a turn round. The answer: pocket, then the next one round; the egg-bearers drop theirs in the pocket just left, clear of the pillars. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host laid the table edge to edge with snares, two courses, leaving three clean plates bare. We moved plate to plate round her. The two given eggs set them down on the plate just finished. Well cleared, at least.",
      oathbroker: "Terms: the floor is forfeit but for three clear pockets, twice. We moved from one to the next round her. The fine print: those carrying eggs must drop them in the pocket they leave, never the one ahead or behind a pillar. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "She filled the web with snares. Three quiet places. Then three more, a step round. We moved together. Those with eggs left them where they had stood. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Snares on every inch but three holes, then three more a turn round. Took the far hole, then the next. Had the eggs once: waited, dropped it where I stood, moved on. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark wove snares over everything, leaving three bare pockets, then three more, a turn along. I slipped from one to the next behind her. Given eggs, I left them in the pocket I was leaving, as one does. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Snares everywhere, twice, and only three holes to stand in! I kept casting in mine, then hopped round to the next. They hung eggs on me, so I dropped the lot where I'd been. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: three clear pockets; then three more, a turn round her. Step. Hot: eggs on my back, the second layer coming. Dropped them where I stood, ran to the next pocket. Back to the middle after.",
      driftblade: "Snares from wall to wall and only three holes, so I went hole to hole round her, through the gap the first layer left, and the eggs on me dropped where I'd been, and I was already gone.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady strings the whole stage with snares, leaving three bare marks, then three more a step round. The company moves mark to mark. The two carrying eggs leave them on the mark just struck. (The Doll took the inner mark.)",
      cleric: "She filled the web with snares and left only three clear pockets, then three more a little round. We went from one to the next. The two she laid eggs on left them where they'd stood, out of everyone's way. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "She sowed the whole field with snares, leaving three clearings, then three more a turn on. My lot moved clearing to clearing. Those carrying eggs left them in the clearing behind, not the one ahead. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Two layers of snares, a few beats apart; the second's pockets show early, a turn round. I'll have stepped round on the beat. The egg-carriers drop theirs in the pocket just left. I did, this time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: snares over the whole web, twice, with three clear pockets each. Remedy: pocket, then the next one round her. Note: the egg pools go in the pocket just left, never ahead or behind a pillar. Spoiled floor, labelled.",
      threadweaver: "Snares stitched over every inch, then a second layer, three holes each. I walked my little stitches round from one to the next. The two with eggs dropped them in the hole they'd left. A neat seam.",
    } },
  },
};
